TEXT 232 224 Left 2 !.tran 0 1 0 0.1ms
TEXT 840 -24 Left 2 ;To compare with the RMS values of the .ac simulation\nSet the x-Axis value to start at approx. 160ms = swing in finished\nWith  STRG+Click to the tracename the RMS is shown
TEXT 16 -616 Left 6 ;Capacitances and inductances of normal line model and \ncoupled line models:\n \nNormal line:\nCb1,2,3=Ce1+3*Cl1(cable Cl=0, Cb=Ce)\nL1,2,3=L1,L2,L3\nCoupled line:\nCE1=C11-C12-C13\nCE2=C22-C12-C23 \nCE3=C33-C13-C23\n(C21=C12 C32=C23 C13=C31) \nL1=XL11-0.5*XM12-0.5*XM13\nL2=XL22-0.5*XM12-0.5*XM23\nL3=XL33-0.5*XM13-0.5*XM23\n(XM32=XM23 XM21=XM12 XM31=XM13)\nCoupling factor= 0.5 (in this example)\nk12=XM12/sqrt(XL11*XL22)\nk13=XM13/sqrt(XL11*XL33)\nk23=XM23/sqrt(XL22*XL33)
TEXT 712 -544 Left 6 ;Example:\nNormal line:\nCB1=10.003n CE1=10n -> CL1=CL12=(CB1-CE1)/3=1p (CL2=CL23,CL3=CL13)\nL1=1/314.159 XB=1ohm\nCoupled line:\nPhase1:\nC11=10.002n C12=1p C13=1p -> CE1=C11-C12-C13=10n CL12=1p CL13=1p\nXM12=1 XM13=1 XL11=2\nL1=2-0.5*1-0.5*1=1ohm\nCoupling factor:\nk12=1/sqrt(2*2)=0.5\nwhich is all ident with above for the symmetric case
TEXT 232 216 Left 2 !.param tdv=1 tdt=1  fn1=50 omega_n={2*pi*fn1}
